package part6;
import java.util.*;
//A leader is an element that is greater than all the elements to its right.
public class Leaders_In_An_Array {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
            int [] numbers = {23,10,88,9,234,1};
            int numbersLength = numbers.length;
            List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();
            
            for(int i=0; i< numbersLength; i++){
                boolean flag = true;
                for(int j=i+1; j< numbersLength; j++){
                    if(numbers[j] > numbers[i])
                        {
                            flag = false;
                            break;
                        }
                }
                if(flag){
                    result.add(numbers[i]);
                    }
                    
            }
          System.out.println(result);
    }
}


/*

if (isLeader) line only runs after the inner loop finishes, 
    whether that's because it completed naturally (ran out of j values) or because it broke early. 
    It always runs — but what matters is what isLeader holds at that point.
    
--> isLeader needs a fresh start for every new i, because each element's "is it a leader?" question 
        is independent of the others. If declared inside the outer 
        loop → resets to true every round → fair, clean check each time.


